      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "c-NuG5uVc22-6u2i9XCOt",
      "originalText": "PostgreSQL: Row-Level Locks vs Isolation Levels\n\n→ These operate at different layers but work together to manage concurrency\n\n\n🔒 Row-Level Locks\n\n→ Explicit, manual locks on specific rows\n→ Acquired via SELECT ... FOR UPDATE or SELECT ... FOR SHARE\n→ Surgical and immediate — you're directly saying \"don't let others touch this row\"\n→ Control who can modify a row concurrently\n\n\n👁️ Isolation Levels\n\n→ Implicit, automatic guarantees enforced by the DB engine\n→ Set via SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...\n→ Control what data your transaction can see\n→ Handled internally using MVCC (snapshot versioning) — no actual locks held in most cases\n\n\nLevel                                       Dirty Read           Non-repeatable Read          Phantom Read\nRead Committed (default)         ✗                                 possible                         possible\nRepeatable Read                        ✗                                    ✗                                    ✗ (in PG)\nSerializable                                 ✗                                    ✗                                    ✗\n\n\nKey Distinction\n\n→ Isolation levels protect reads\n→ Row-level locks protect write coordination\n→ In practice, you often need both (e.g. inventory updates, booking systems)\n\n\nPractical Example: Two transactions read + update the same row\n\n→ REPEATABLE READ alone ensures a consistent snapshot, but won't stop the second writer from clobbering the first ⚠️\n→ SELECT FOR UPDATE blocks transaction 2 until transaction 1 commits — so transaction 2 reads the updated value before proceeding ✅\n\n\n⚔️ Tradeoff: SELECT FOR UPDATE vs SERIALIZABLE\n\n                                    SELECT FOR UPDATE                           SERIALIZABLE\nApproach                    Pessimistic — blocks                          Optimistic — aborts\nMechanism                     Explicit lock                                 SSI (tracks rw-anti-dependency cycles)\nOn conflict                One waits, both succeed                  One succeeds, one must retry\nOverhead                     Lock contention                                Dependency tracking\n\n\n→ SERIALIZABLE uses SSI — lets both transactions run freely, then aborts one if a dangerous read/write dependency cycle is detected\n→ Error thrown: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions\n→ Your app must implement retry logic to handle aborts\n\n\nWhen to choose which:\n\nSELECT FOR UPDATE\n→ more predictable for high-contention rows (inventory counts, seat booking); one waits, both complete\n\nSERIALIZABLE\n→ gives correctness automatically, but you trade blocking for unexpected aborts and retry complexity",
